From: j7
Subject: j7: never refuse a GPU submit the compositor needs

The 900 MHz GPU-on boot ends at 12 submits, every one rc=0, and then the render server never draws
again and nothing more reaches the log.  Three things in the client path can end a session exactly
like that, and all three are here:

 - `gJ7M.inflight` was one flag for the whole GPU, so a chain started on slot 1 made the next chain
   on slot 0 a refusal, and Mesa turns a refused submit into a failed batch.  In-flight is tracked
   per slot now, the wait covers every slot that has a chain, and a refused submit drains only that
   slot and tries once more before answering the client.

 - a START the job manager did not take stays latched in JS_COMMAND_NEXT, and every later submit to
   that slot is refused forever with nothing in the log.  The latch is cleared and the submit goes
   through.

 - a failed `j7m_recover()` after four bad chains (or one MMU fault) set `gJ7MServing = false`, and
   from then on every submit returned kIOReturnNotReady: a client that by design never draws again.
   The service stays up and the next submit retries the reset, core power and AS0 program.

What the GPU draws is unchanged: this is the client end of the Panfrost bridge, not a new renderer.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

